"*you’ll have to manually check for new Whonix releases and* `manually "
"install them from source code <https://www.whonix.org/wiki/Dev/"
"Build_Documentation>`__ *.*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/debian-and-whonix-update-troubleshooting.rst:88
#: 65385d69cf3d40d8af1e271e0d42e126
msgid "Never Install Unsigned Packages"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/debian-and-whonix-update-troubleshooting.rst:91
#: e07097c19a3d4e519a1291cf5cedacd9
msgid "If you see something like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/debian-and-whonix-update-troubleshooting.rst:101
#: da9f2fc76c6e43f58332687176789005
msgid ""
"Don’t proceed! Press ``N`` and ``<enter>``. Running ``apt-get update`` again "
"should fix it. If not, something is broken or it’s a `Man in the middle "
"attack <https://www.whonix.org/wiki/Warning#Man-in-the-middle_attacks>`__, "
"which isn’t that unlikely, since we are updating over Tor exit relays and "
"some of them are malicious. Try to `change your Tor circuit <https://www."
"whonix.org/wiki/Arm#Arm>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/debian-and-whonix-update-troubleshooting.rst:104
#: bd162d8e835b4d7483da0ccf4d68e39e
msgid "Signature Verification Warnings"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/debian-and-whonix-update-troubleshooting.rst:107
#: a09515bf8f6c4fcc94adfce6b51f468a
msgid ""
"There should be none at the moment. If there was such a warning, it would "
"look like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/debian-and-whonix-update-troubleshooting.rst:115
#: aa187257471a478290a313fa0574944a
msgid ""
"Even though, ``apt-get`` will automatically ignore repositories with expired "
"keys or signatures, you will not receive upgrades from that repository. "
"Unless the issue is already known/documented, it should be reported so it "
"can be further investigated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/debian-and-whonix-update-troubleshooting.rst:117
#: 6d3458eca49f465986dce11b8a93c293
msgid ""
"There are two possible reasons why this could happen, either there is an "
"issue with the repository that the maintainers have to fix, or you are "
"victim of a `Man-in-the-middle_attacks <https://www.whonix.org/wiki/"
"Warning#Man-in-the-middle_attacks>`__. The latter would not be a big issue "
"and might go away after a while automatically or try to `change your Tor "
"circuit <https://www.whonix.org/wiki/Arm#Arm>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/debian-and-whonix-update-troubleshooting.rst:119
#: d586f58e4aa24811a4e9456f8b2b574b
msgid ""
"In past various apt repositories were signed with expired key: `The Tor "
"Project’s apt repository key was expired <https://trac.torproject.org/"
"projects/tor/ticket/12994>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/debian-and-whonix-update-troubleshooting.rst:121
#: 1c893bbb04fc49bb86df223a89e94899
msgid "You saw the following warning:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/debian-and-whonix-update-troubleshooting.rst:132
#: 6bf3b2f7272a4af88777e8498963357d
msgid ""
"It had already been `reported <https://trac.torproject.org/projects/tor/"
"ticket/12994>`__. There was no immediate danger. You could have just ignored "
"it. Just make sure, you never install unsigned packages as explained above."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/debian-and-whonix-update-troubleshooting.rst:134
#: 3488683027cc4312ba16172d7f8ab215
msgid ""
"If you were to see other signature verification errors, those should be "
"reported, but it shouldn’t happen at this time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/debian-and-whonix-update-troubleshooting.rst:137
#: 20a56eda72e9407c8a0dd255893eeff9
msgid "Changed Configuration Files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/debian-and-whonix-update-troubleshooting.rst:140
#: 0c5994cae453479d8ed5ac10bb7ff6c0
msgid "If you see something like the following."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/debian-and-whonix-update-troubleshooting.rst:158
#: f2a12dc1e67f493cb02bb912a7c164ee
msgid ""
"Be careful. If the updated file isn’t coming from Whonix specific package "
"(some are called ``whonix-...``), then press ``n``. Otherwise anonymity/"
"privacy/security settings deployed with Whonix might get lost. If you are an "
"advanced user and know better, you can of course manually check the "
"difference and merge them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/debian-and-whonix-update-troubleshooting.rst:160
#: 38434ce8ace543c99ce07f11c50907bd
msgid ""
"How could you find out if the file is coming from a Whonix specific package "
"or not?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/debian-and-whonix-update-troubleshooting.rst:162
#: b17eaccb2f1e483fbed7a21f994137cb
msgid ""
"Whonix specific packages are sometimes called ``whonix-...``. In the example "
"above it’s saying ``Setting up ifupdown ...``, so the file isn’t coming from "
"a Whonix specific package. In this case, you should press ``n`` as advised "
"in the paragraph above."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/troubleshooting/debian-and-whonix-update-troubleshooting.rst:164
#: 6d1921023fc947c2a5ce42a0e00e75d1
msgid ""
"If the package name does include ``whonix-...``, it’s a Whonix specific "
"package. In that case, your safest bet should be pressing ``y``, but then "
"you would lose your customized settings. You can re-add them afterwards. "
"Such conflicts will hopefully rarely happen, if you use `Whonix modular "
"flexible .d style configuration folders <https://www.whonix.org/wiki/"
"Whonix_Configuration_Files>`__."
msgstr ""
